  "query_preview": "..\n     Strong winds and dry conditions will bring widespread critical to \nextreme fire weather conditions on Saturday...\n\nSaturday and Saturday night: The well advertised cold front is \nknocking on the door of the Northwest and currently approaching the \n130W longitudinal line in the eastern Pacific. A deep upper level \nlow is fueling this frontal system and it's progged to sweep across \nthe Inland Northwest tomorrow in the afternoon. The combination of a \nfairly deep surface low spinning up east of the Great Divide in \nAlberta and strong cold air advection will result in strong winds \nacross the Inland Northwest. Moisture with the front will all be \nsqueezed out west of the Cascade crest leaving a dry frontal passage \nfor eastern Washington and North Idaho.\n\n* Winds: A potent west to east surface gradient will develop across \n  the region on Saturday resulting in strong sustained winds up to \n  20-30 mph and locally up to 35 mph over north-central Washington \n  and the lower Garfield/Asotin counties in the Northeast Blues. \n  Widespread wind gusts of 35-45 mph is expected and could locally \n  gust as high as 50 mph for these windier locations.\n\n* Fire Weather Impacts: The primary concern with this cold front\n  passage is for rapid fire spread for any new or ongoing fires.\n  All areas are going to be at risk of seeing at least breezy\n  conditions and most being quite gusty. The combination of\n  gusty winds and dry conditions will bring critical fire\n  conditions regionwide, and areas Wenatchee to the Waterville\n  Plateau, Colville Reservation, and Columbia Basin to the\n  Spokane Area, Palouse, and Snake River Valley will see the\n  potential for extreme fire weather conditions with the wind\n  speeds predicted. The Fire Weather Watch has been upgraded to\n  a Red Flag Warning and the aforementioned areas of extreme\n  fire weather has the Particularly Dangerous Situation (PDS)\n  attached to them. This indicates the rare situation in which\n  fuels are extreme dry in combination of an anomalously windy,\n  dry event. Winds also look to shift northwesterly in the \n  evening on Saturday primarily across the northwest portion of \n  the forecast area.\n\n* Secondary Impacts: Winds will also be capable of minor tree damage \n  that could lead to isolated power outages. Another secondary \n  concern to watch for will be for blowing dust across the Columbia \n  Basin. We aren't in the blowing dust season when farm fields are \n  being planted, so any blowing dust is expected to be patchy.\n\nSunday into Monday: Fire weather concerns will continue into Sunday \nwith winds remaining breezy. Not nearly as windy as Saturday, but \nstill dry and enough for at least elevated fire weather impacts. The \nlongwave trough of lower pressure pushes into the Northwest on \nSunday and a backside shortwave also swings across during this time. \nWinds switch northerly down the Okanogan Valley Sunday night into \nMonday and increase at 15-25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph. Winds pick \nup overnight as relative humidity values recover. The northerly \nwinds look to weaken on Monday.\n\nMonday night through Thursday: Winds look to continue to relax next \nweek under dry northwesterly flow. Fires will likely remain active \nwithout any moisture, and we will see northern winds pushing smoke \nfrom fires burning across north-central Washington and in British \nColumbia south. This may result in more smoke and a degradation of \nair quality across the Columbia Basin, into the Spokane \nArea/Palouse, and in the Idaho Panhandle. Temperatures will decrease \nbriefly in the low 80s on Monday, but then warm back up again back \ninto the upper 80s and 90s Tuesday through Friday. /SVH",
